Question stem:
An insurance company sells a special two-year term survival insurance with end-of-term payout to both smokers (S) and non-smokers (NS), with the following information provided: 
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)] 
\item The death payout is 100,000 yuan; the end-of-term payout is 30,000 yuan; 
\item The level annual premium for non-smokers' policies is determined on an equivalence basis; 
\item The annual premium for smokers' policies is twice that of non-smokers' policies; 
\item The mortality intensity for non-smokers \(\mu^{\rm NS}_{x+t}=0.1\), \(t>0\); 
\item The annual mortality rate for smokers \(\qx{x+k}[S]=1.5\,\qx{x+k}[NS]\), \(k=0,1\); 
\item The annual interest rate \(i=0.08\). 
\end{enumerate} 
Calculate the expected value of the random variable representing the loss present value at the time of issuance of the smokers' policy.

For a special whole life insurance policy for a 40-year-old insured, with a face amount of 100,000, it is known that: 
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)] 
\item The death benefit is paid as a lump sum upon death; 
\item Equal annual premiums are paid monthly, for a maximum term of 20 years; 
\item Mortality rates follow the standard ultimate life table; 
\item Annual interest rate \(i=0.05\); 
\item Deaths are assumed to be uniformly distributed within each age year; 
\item Initial expenses are 200; 
\item Subsequent annual expenses are 4\% of each premium, including the first year; 
\item Premiums are determined by the equivalence principle. 
\end{enumerate} Calculate the monthly gross premium for this policy.
